 Countermeasure: Disclosure of interests and strict adherence to conflict-of-interest policies are essential to maintaining fairness in decision-making.";}i:2;i:1130;}i:16;a:3:{i:0;s:6:"p_open";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:1130;}i:17;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:31:"Delaying the Resolution Process";}i:2;i:1651;}i:18;a:3:{i:0;s:7:"p_close";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:1682;}i:19;a:3:{i:0;s:12:"preformatted";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:493:" Tactic: CoC members might stall decision-making or the approval of resolution plans to exert pressure on the debtor or resolution applicants, often to negotiate better terms for themselves.
 Beneficiary: This tactic can benefit members who may receive higher payouts or more favorable terms as a result of prolonged negotiations.
 Countermeasure: Timelines set by the IBC must be strictly adhered to, with oversight from the National Company Law Tribunal (NCLT) to prevent unnecessary delays.";}i:2;i:1682;}i:20;a:3:{i:0;s:6:"p_open";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:1682;}i:21;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:35:"Manipulating Information Disclosure";}i:2;i:2183;}i:22;a:3:{i:0;s:7:"p_close";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:2218;}i:23;a:3:{i:0;s:12:"preformatted";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:491:" Tactic: Members may selectively disclose or withhold crucial information about the corporate debtor's financial health, influencing other members’ votes and decisions on resolution plans.
